      "description": "YAKUYOKE is a photo-based glitch/generative art work.\n\n- YAKU (厄): misfortune or calamity\n- YOKE (除け): to ward off or get rid of.\n\nIt is a ceremony held at Japanese shrines to shake off bad luck and misfortune and wish for good fortune. \n\nThe theme of the project was this: \"Can I express the flame to ward off misfortune and calamity?\"\n\nThe act of burning eight pure street photographs taken in Shibuya, Tokyo, and making them non-transferable corresponds to the act of burning firewood. \n\nUsing p5.js and WEBGL programs, the Burned photo data is randomly retrieved by Recursion, and various glitch destructions are added to reproduce the flames of burning bad luck. \n\nAll eight photographic inscriptions are Burned, making them non-transferable and non-sellable. The generative art program is inscribe on Uncommon Sats. Programs and photos are in a Parent-Child provenance.",
